  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThe upper low embedded in the broader western CONUS upper trough \nwill begin to eject NE toward the central plains states Monday while \ndragging across NW TX only a small piece of shortwave energy. Monday \nnight into Tuesday. Height fields over the eastern Pacific will \nremain deflated with a weaker but still unstable SW flow pattern \naloft left over W TX Tuesday. A pooling of Gulf moisture begins to \nmove onshore by this time as low level winds become less easterly, \nbut the amount of inland progress models show the moisture flux \nmaking looks to be little too late to connect up with the passing \nshortwave to the NW. What that has left us is a fairly dry/more \nstable pattern over the Hill Country & Central TX with a more moist \nand/or unstable set up for Coastal Prairies and part of the Rio \nGrande Plains for Tuesday. The Edwards Plateau will see its better \nrain chances early Tuesday from the shortwave, but will probably go \nstable in the afternoon before the deeper moisture can arrive. All \nthat said there could be a good chance (40-60%) for some rain over \nthe Coastal Prairies/Rio Grande Plains areas situated south of San \nAntonio metro. \n\nWhile much attention was spent discussing somewhat uninspiring rain \nchances for Tuesday, the best chances for rain in the next 7 days \nare forecast for Thursday and Friday after a fairly low coverage \nafternoon Wednesday. Wednesday into Wednesday night the tilted ridge \nover TX continues to flatten eastward leaving a deep 700 mb to \ntropopause pattern of SW flow to connect the rest of TX with some of \nthat tropical moisture from the eastern Pacific. There will be \nconsiderable uncertainty with what happens with Vance but the \ngeneral expectation is for slow movement and a gradual weakening by \nearly next week. Most of the latest runs, including the AI runs, \nhold the system together and well off the coast of Mexico to be \ncapable of feeding TX a portion of its energy/moisture as it \nencounters that SW shear aloft. While our pattern shows no \nindications of a surface feature to greatly enhance rain chances, \nthe rise in PWat values to potentially over 1.8 inches over South TX \ncould lead to a variety of rain chances, some connected with \ninstability passing through in the NW flow aloft and others moving \ninland as feeder showers and storms. Our current forecast highlights \nwestern counties with the best chances at 50-75%, mainly 25-50% over \nthe I-35 corridor and Coastal Prairies for both of these days. \nConfidence of this pattern holding together lowers significantly \nafter Friday.\n\nTemperatures remain warm and steady through next week with daytime \nhighs mostly ranging from the mid 70s to the mid 80s. Pending how \nthick cloud cover and how much rain coverage becomes, a day or two \nmay trend a little lower. Overnights remain mild and a bit humid \nwith lows ranging mostly from the upper 50s to the mid 60s.",
